        Description: The vertical sky region: disconnected floating platforms, clouds, and moving lifts suspended over a fall-away void, demanding precise jumps as the route climbs upward in pursuit of the kidnapper.
        Plan for this region: This region is the sky stretch of the platformer, occupying the 50 m span from x=105 to x=155 between the underground and castle stretches, read as a continuous left-to-right traversal that also climbs upward, with all action confined to a shallow playable plane near the front face of the slab (the small z-depth) so movement lies in the vertical x-y plane and the painted backdrop band behind it stays unobstructed. Unlike the earlier stretches there is no continuous ground baseline here: the lower portion of the region is an open fall-away void with no walkable floor, so the disconnected platforms are the only footing and the threat of falling drives the precise jumping that defines this stretch. The route enters at the lower-left near x=105, picking up from the rising exit passage of the underground stretch so the player arrives at a modest height on the left edge, then ascends rightward across a loosely spaced field of separate floating surfaces — small solid platforms and stepping blocks of varying size, rounded cartoon cloud platforms that read as soft but solid footholds, and lifts that translate along fixed paths (some rising and falling vertically, some sliding horizontally) to bridge gaps too wide to clear with a standing jump. These elements are arranged as a staggered upward staircase of footholds, trending higher in y as the traversal moves right, with the lower-left entry kept comparatively forgiving and the spacing of gaps widening and the reliance on moving lifts increasing toward the right to escalate difficulty. Collectible coins trace the natural jump arcs between platforms to mark the intended path, floating item blocks and breakable blocks sit at jumpable points along the way, and a sparse scatter of platformer enemies sits or patrols on the larger stationary platforms to pressure the landings. Decorative non-solid clouds may dress the open air at heights and depths set back from the playable plane to fill the void without blocking the route. The traversal terminates at the right edge near x=155 at an elevated foothold that delivers the player toward the entry of the adjoining castle stretch, so the climb resolves into the castle approach. Throughout, footholds are positioned so that each is reachable from the previous by a jump or a ride on a moving lift, keeping the upward route continuous and readable while the empty void below makes the precision of each leap the central character of the stretch.
