        Plan for this region: This region is the underground cavern stretch of the platformer, occupying the 45 m span between the overworld and sky stretches, read as a continuous left-to-right traversal confined to a shallow playable plane near the front face of the slab (the small z-depth) so all action lies in that vertical x-y plane. The route enters at the lower-left where a descending green pipe deposits the player down from the overworld floor above, then crosses the cavern as an enclosed corridor of stone-and-dirt floor segments broken by several gaps and pits the player must jump, with the floor sitting low in the region. A band of brick-type and solid blocks runs overhead as a ceiling that visually caps the cavern and tightens the vertical clearance, making jumps more precise than in the open overworld; some of these overhead blocks double as hittable platforms. Between floor and ceiling, free-floating block clusters and short platforms sit at jumpable heights to bridge the gaps, and rows of collectible coins trace the natural jump arcs over the pits and along the platform paths to guide forward movement. Tucked above the brick ceiling or set back in small recessed alcoves off the main path are hidden bonus pockets — concealed chambers rewarding exploration — reachable by breaking up through the ceiling band or veering off the direct route. Patrolling ground enemies and shelled creatures move along the flat floor segments between gaps, and the spacing of obstacles is tighter than the overworld to escalate difficulty. The traversal trends gently upward across the span and terminates at the right edge near x=105 with an exit pipe or rising passage that delivers the player out higher than they entered, setting up the climb into the adjoining sky stretch. The overall enclosed cavern character — darker stone surfaces, a continuous overhead ceiling, and constrained vertical space — distinguishes it clearly from the open grassy opening while preserving readable left-to-right sightlines.
